course: dictionary
lesson: 10567
title: Uncommon: rare or remarkable
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Uncommon usually means not seen or happening often. An uncommon animal is one you rarely spot, and an uncommon plant is one that does not grow everywhere. The un- at the start is a clue: it turns common into its opposite.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. In a story, the Mock Turtle says it «sounds uncommon nonsense». Here uncommon does not mean rare. In old-fashioned talk it means very, so the nonsense is very great nonsense.
explain: Read the words around the word to find which meaning fits. Ask: is the sentence about how often something happens, or about how great it is? If it is about how often, uncommon means rare. If it is about how great, it means remarkable, as in «an uncommon talent».
explain: Everyday talk uses it too. «It's not uncommon» means it happens quite often. Always try your meaning in the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
fact: Uncommon means not happening or seen often; rare or unusual.
fact: Uncommon can also mean remarkable or very great, as in «an uncommon talent». This meaning is old-fashioned.
fact: «Sounds uncommon nonsense» means sounds very great nonsense in old-fashioned talk.
fact: «It's not uncommon» means it happens quite often.
fact: The un- at the start of a word often means "not", so uncommon is the opposite of common.
exercise: What does uncommon mean in «an uncommon talent»? | Ordinary, found everywhere | *Remarkable, very great | Sleepy and slow | Loud and noisy
exercise: Ben says, «Frogs like this are uncommon in our town.» What does uncommon tell you? | *Frogs like this are seldom seen there | Frogs like this are seen every day | Frogs like this are very loud | Frogs like this can fly
exercise: «It's not uncommon for it to rain here in April.» What does this mean? | *It happens quite often | It rains only at night | It rains only once a year | It never rains in April
test: The Mock Turtle says «sounds uncommon nonsense». What does uncommon mean here? | *Very great, very much | Rare, seen only now and then | Sad and tired | Quiet and calm
test: Which word is the opposite of uncommon in its everyday meaning? | Strange | Rare | *Common | Unusual
exam: Mia reads «an uncommon talent» and says uncommon means rare, like an uncommon bird. Is she right? | Yes, always | Nobody knows | No, it means ordinary | *Partly: here it means remarkable, so the context matters
